WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.763
Azure private endpoints are the most common
source of private-address split-brain.

2
00:00:04.763 --> 00:00:09.020
When you create one, Azure writes
its record into a private zone,

3
00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:11.476
and public DNS points into that name.

4
00:00:11.476 --> 00:00:13.626
You only get the private answer if your

5
00:00:13.626 --> 00:00:16.113
query reaches a resolver
linked to that zone.

6
00:00:16.113 --> 00:00:17.625
So the rule is simple.

7
00:00:17.625 --> 00:00:21.958
Every origin, whether on-premises,
another cloud or the branch,

8
00:00:21.958 --> 00:00:26.800
forwards the service's public zone
to the Azure DNS Private Resolver.

9
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:28.075
Not the private zone.

10
00:00:28.075 --> 00:00:30.840
And Azure Government uses
different zone names,

11
00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.913
so commercial rules don't carry over.

12
00:00:32.913 --> 00:00:36.242
The Private Resolver is a
bridge, not an authority,

13
00:00:36.242 --> 00:00:41.276
so you reach it by forwarding, never by
delegation, and deploy one per region.

14
00:00:41.276 --> 00:00:43.525
Addresses need the same discipline,

15
00:00:43.525 --> 00:00:48.625
with the enterprise IPAM as the authority
and the clouds reconciled against it.

16
00:00:48.625 --> 00:00:51.650
Put it together, and you
get one governed path.
